<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zinder</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zinder is a city in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and the country's second-largest. The capital of the Sultanate of Damagaram from the mid-18th century, it grew as a stop on the caravan route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, and its old town and sultan's palace survive. It was occupied by the French in 1899 and served as the capital of their Niger territory until 1926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zinder.jpg" alt="Zinder" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mab.black · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zinder_centre_ville.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zinder lies 861 km east of the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niamey">Niamey</a> and 240 km north of the Nigerian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>. Locally it is also called Damagaram. The centre consists of three main areas. In the north is Zengou, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zango">Zango</a>, the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> suburb, known for its vernacular architecture. In the south lies Birni, the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> town, which holds the Zinder Great Mosque, Fort Tanimoune, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s Palace and a museum. Between them is Sabon Gari, the new town, which is the commercial centre and has a large market, the Grand Marché. The city has many distinctive granite rocks and a long history of water shortages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was first a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> village called Zengou. The town grew greatly in importance after Kanuri aristocrats arrived in 1736, built a fortified quarter named Birni to the south and made Zinder the capital of the Sultanate of Damagaram. It became an important centre of the trans-Saharan trade, with routes south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>. The sultanate stayed nominally subject to the Borno Empire until the mid-to-late 19th century, when Sultan Tanimoune Dan Souleymane declared independence and began a period of vigorous expansion.</p><p>Europeans reached the region in the 19th century, beginning with the German explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a>, who stayed in Zinder in 1851. The French explorer Marius Gabriel Cazemajou was killed in the city in 1897. After a brief resistance Zinder was occupied in July 1899 by Lt. Pallier of the Voulet-Chanoine mission. The French barracks was called Fort Cazemajou, and it served as the capital of the Niger Military Territory when that was created in 1911. In 1926 the capital was moved back to the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niamey">Niamey</a>, after fears of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> revolts and as relations improved with the Djerma of the west. After Niger became independent the fort was renamed for Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanimoune">Tanimoune</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city's economy rests largely on the agriculture of its surroundings. It is divided into five urban municipalities, Zinder I to Zinder V, and has an airport a few kilometres to the southwest and the André-Salifou University, founded in 2010. The oil refinery SORAZ, commissioned in November 2011, stands 50 km to the north and was the country's first.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zinder” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French conquest of the area began with the Voulet-Chanoine mission of 1899, notorious for massacring villagers, and the occupation of <b>Zinder</b>, capital of the Sultanate of Damagaram.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a></li><li><blockquote>It began as a small vassal state of Bornu, and in the mid-18th century its rulers moved their seat to <b>Zinder</b>, which became the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-damagaram">Sultanate of Damagaram</a></li></ul></section>
